MS 0410 [1]

Volume 1 – General Administration MS 0410 – R. 6/1/26 – OMTL – 697 Quality Assurance (QA) Case Review

[ All QA case reviews are completed on Worker Portal in the QA Module. Reviews are completed to assess the accuracy of case processing and to identify error trends and training needs to be addressed on a local, regional, or statewide basis.

A. The purpose of a case review is to ensure that:

1. The case is processed correctly;

2. The decision to approve or deny eligibility is made according to the applicable program policy;

3. All information is obtained, reviewed, and evaluated to substantiate an eligibility decision;

4. Impending changes are identified and processed appropriately;

5. Computer matches are acted upon timely and appropriately;

6. All required forms are scanned to the Electronic Case File (ECF), and all inconsistent information is clarified;

7. All areas of eligibility are documented;

8. All elements affected by an error are identified; and

9. All computations are correct.

B. Reviewer Functions:

1. Reviewers follow guidelines issued by the Service Region Administrator’s (SRA) office, which establishes the required number of cases reviewed monthly.

2. Reviewers use the different levels of reviews available. Each level of review contributes to the assessment of casework and identification of the potential need for coaching, mentoring, training, policy support, practice support, and system changes.

C. Review Process:

At each level of case review, these general procedures are appropriate:

1. Reviewers must read cases and review all elements.

2. When errors are identified, the reviewer cites a specific policy for the error.

MS 0497

[1]

Volume 1 – General Administration MS 0497 – R. 6/1/26 – OMTL – 696 Individual Hearing Rights

[An individual not only has the right to request a hearing but also has additional rights.

A. Explain to the individual or their representative the following rights in a language and format they understand:

1. The right to a qualified interpreter if the individual is Limited English Proficient (LEP) and rights to reasonable modification and auxiliary aids and services for individuals with disabilities;

2. To present the case themselves or to have it presented by legal counsel or another representative. Inform the individual of the availability of free legal services;

3. To review the case record relating to the issue;

4. To bring witnesses to support their case in the hearing;

5. To present arguments without interruption;

6. To question any testimony or evidence and cross-examine witnesses; and

7. To submit evidence establishing pertinent facts and circumstances in the case.

B. Explain to the individual that the department does not provide payment for legal counsel but, if available, will refer them to a legal aid agency.

C. Provide the individual and the individual's representative or legal counsel with adequate opportunity to examine all documents and records to be used at the hearing, a reasonable time before the date of the hearing, as well as during the hearing. The contents of the case file, including the application form and documents of verification used by the agency to establish the household's ineligibility or eligibility and allotment, are made available. Confidential information, such as names of individuals who have disclosed information about the household without its knowledge or the nature or status of pending criminal prosecutions, is protected from release. If requested by the household or its representative, the agency provides a free copy of the relevant portions of the case file. Confidential information protected from release and other documents or records that the household will not otherwise have an opportunity to contest or challenge are not presented at the hearing, and do not affect the hearing officer's decision.

D. If the individual, after requesting a hearing, is dissatisfied with the medical evidence used in making the case decision, the individual may request an examination from another medical examiner. If an examination by an internist or specialist was received within three months prior to the date of the hearing request, the individual must specify the reason for the additional examination.

MS 0810

(1) Volume 1 - General Administration MS 0810 - R. 8/1/26 - OMTL-703 How to Identify a Claim

A. [Identification of claims is an automatic process if an overpayment is detected by the Integrated Eligibility and Enrollment System (IEES) during case processing. For example, field staff make appropriate case changes and IEES detects a potential overpayment. At disposition, an automatic Discrepancy Referral information screen is triggered. Field Staff can create an overpayment discrepancy task for the Regional Claims Worker (RCW) to review from this screen. All other claims are manually referred.

B. A Manual Claim referral is created when the following occurs:

1. Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) benefits are trafficked;

2. Benefits designated for a specific purpose are used to purchase unapproved items or services;

3. Claims identified as occurring prior to implementation of IEES and were not processed on Kentucky Claims Debt (KCD) Management System;

4. Medicaid Fraud;

5. Individual Child Care Fraud;

6. Child Care Provider Fraud;

7. Some supportive service payments are overpaid; or

8. When any overpayment is detected by field staff that is not automatically detected by IEES.

C. When completing a claim referral or while establishing a claim, staff will utilize one of the following detection code associated with how the claim was identified:

1. Batch Match/Computer Match: Used when computer file data from the Income and Eligibility Verification System (IEVS) is matched against case and member information on Worker Portal and the discovery of unreported income/resources results in an overpayment or incorrect issuance of benefits for all Family Support assistance categories;

2. Public Assistance Reporting Information System (PARIS): Used when a recipient match is received showing benefits were issued in more than one state for the same timeframe;

3. Hotline: Used when an overpayment is found due to a citizen, known or anonymous, contacting the Food and Nutrition Administration (FNA) through a Whistleblower Complaint or the Office of Inspector General

MS 0810 (2) (OIG) through a Hotline Complaint with information pertaining to a benefit recipient that may be unreported or unknown to the agency. FNA or OIG will then route these complaints to the designated contact for investigation and dissemination;]

4. Quality Control (QC) reviews: Used once the QC process has been completed and a positive error for an over issuance has been detected;

5. Audit: Used when an overpayment is detected via the evaluation process conducted by the United States Department of Agriculture (USDA), Department of Health and Human Services (DHHS), Auditor of Public Accounts (APA), or the Government Accounting Office (GAO);

6. Quality Assurance (QA) review: Used when an overpayment is found by a supervisor, principal, Case Review Team (CRT), or the Division of Family Support (DFS) during any case review process;

7. Management Evaluation (ME) reviews: Used when an overpayment is detected during the review of SNAP cases conducted by ME staff;

8. Central Office: Used when an overpayment is detected by central office staff or when there is an overpayment involving an employee or Simplified Assistance for the Elderly (SAFE) claims;

9. Worker: Used when an overpayment is found during the course of regular casework via application, recertification, or case processing and the worker generates a claim referral, or while processing Fidelity Information Systems (FIS) fraud alerts;

10. Office of the Inspector General (OIG): Used when an overpayment is detected during an OIG investigation;

11. Client: Used when an overpayment is found from a report from the household;

12. Department of Child Support Services (DCSS): Used when an overpayment is found due to unreported child support income and DCSS informs the Department for Community Based Services (DCBS) of the income;

13. Third Party: Used when an overpayment is detected from information provided by a source outside of the agency (i.e., an employer);

14. Eligibility Advisor (EA): Used when an overpayment is detected by unreported income discovered via EA; or

15. National Accuracy Clearinghouse (NAC): Used when an overpayment is detected by the receipt of a notification from the NAC system for the receipt of duplicate benefits in more than one state for the same timeframe.]

MS 0810 (3) D. Claims may also be identified by:

1. Changes – An individual reports a change, but after the worker verifies the situation, it is discovered the change was not reported timely;

2. Analysis of expenses vs. income – An individual’s expenses exceed the income, which may be an indicator of unreported income;

3. Worker Interview – A thorough interview increases the likelihood of the applicant reporting other income, such as contributions from family members or friends, which may not be counted in the case;

4. Income – Check stubs reflect an increase due to a change in pay rates or overtime worked;

5. Deductions given in error - such as the Standard Utility Allowance (SUA), Basic Utility Allowance (BUA), 30 and 1/3 deduction;

6. Whistleblower complaints; or

7. SNAP Simplified Reporting (SR) Households – Determine:

a. If the household’s monthly gross income exceeds the allowed limit for the household size; or b. If any member of the household, age 18 through 64, failed to report working fewer than 20 hours a week (paid or unpaid) or participating in the Employment and Training Program (E&T) to meet the time limit work requirements.

E. [When completing a review to determine the overpayment period for claims for establishment, use the policy that was in effect at the time the claim occurred.]

MS 1020

(1) Volume 1 – General Administration MS 1020 – R. 8/1/26 – OMTL-703 Trafficking and Retail Fraud

A. Trafficking is the act of:

1. Buying, selling, stealing, or exchanging Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) benefits, SNAP Electronic Benefit Transfer (EBT) cards, card numbers, Personal Identification Numbers (PIN), or manual vouchers for cash or consideration other than eligible food either directly, indirectly, in complicity or collusion with others, or acting alone;

2. Attempting to buy, sell, steal, or otherwise affect an exchange of SNAP benefits issued and accessed via EBT cards, card numbers, and PIN, or by manual voucher and signatures, for cash or consideration other than eligible food, either directly, indirectly, in complicity or collusion with others, or acting alone;

3. [Exchanging an EBT card and/or card information or SNAP benefits for firearms, ammunition, explosives, or controlled substances. The Office of Inspector General (OIG) identifies and refers all cases involving firearms, ammunition, explosives, or controlled substances to the Kentucky State Police (KSP);]

4. Purchasing a product with SNAP benefits that has a container requiring a return deposit with the intent of obtaining cash by discarding the product and returning the container for the deposit amount, intentionally discarding the product, and intentionally returning the container for the deposit amount;

5. Intentionally reselling or exchanging eligible food purchased with SNAP benefits for the purpose of obtaining cash and/or other considerations; or

6. Retailers knowingly purchasing products originally purchased with SNAP benefits in exchange for cash or considerations other than eligible food.

B. Trafficking hotline referrals are sent from OIG to the Integrity and Analysis Section (IAS). IAS will refer trafficking hotline referrals for individuals to Regional Claims Workers (RCW) for investigation. If EBT account transactions indicate trafficking or misuse of benefits, follow the trafficking procedures outlined in D of this section, or MS 1025 for EBT misuse. The claim is only entered on the Benefit Recovery module when one of the following requirements has been met:

1. The recipient signs and dates form FS-80A (TR), Voluntary Waiver of SNAP Administrative Disqualification Hearing (ADH); 2. An Intentional Program Violation (IPV) is granted by an ADH;

